Subject: Release no login on the readiness of a service manager that is stopping

When the last session closes while the service manager is still coming
up, the manager is told to stop, but its readiness pipe stays open. A new
login that arrives meanwhile waits in srv_pending for the old manager to
exit. If the stopping manager then reports ready, the pipe handler runs
the "ready" job, and its exit sends MSG_OK_DONE to every session of the
login, which releases the new login against services that are being
stopped. The session starts on a bus that disappears a moment later.

Drop the readiness of a manager once it is no longer the login's
srv_pid; the next manager reports on its own pipe. Also, when a manager
starts while the previous manager's pipe is still open (the previous
manager ended without reporting), close that pipe and take it off the
poll list. The pipe handler finds a login only by its current pipe, and
an event on the old one would otherwise make turnstiled exit.
---
--- a/src/turnstiled.cc
+++ b/src/turnstiled.cc
@@ -158,6 +158,37 @@
         lgn.remove_sdir();
         return false;
     }
+    /* the readiness pipe of a manager that ended without reporting is
+     * still open; it is dropped from the poll list here, since the pipe
+     * handler knows a login only by its current pipe. The entry is moved
+     * to the end of the pipe area before it is cleared: a restart from the
+     * reaper runs before this iteration's pipe loop, which reads the area
+     * as the first npipes entries, and a hole inside it would hand the
+     * last pipe to the connection handler
+     */
+    if (lgn.userpipe >= 0) {
+        if (!lgn.pipe_queued) {
+            std::size_t last = npipes + 1;
+            for (std::size_t j = 2; j < fds.size(); ++j) {
+                if (fds[j].fd != lgn.userpipe) {
+                    continue;
+                }
+                if ((j < last) && (last < fds.size())) {
+                    pollfd tmp = fds[j];
+                    fds[j] = fds[last];
+                    fds[last] = tmp;
+                    j = last;
+                }
+                fds[j].fd = -1;
+                fds[j].revents = 0;
+                --npipes;
+                break;
+            }
+        }
+        close(lgn.userpipe);
+        lgn.userpipe = -1;
+        lgn.pipe_queued = false;
+    }
     print_dbg("srv: create readiness pipe");
     unlinkat(lgn.dirfd, "ready", 0);
     if (mkfifoat(lgn.dirfd, "ready", 0700) < 0) {
@@ -1226,8 +1257,14 @@
         /* unlink the pipe */
         unlinkat(lgn->dirfd, "ready", 0);
         print_dbg("pipe: gone");
-        /* wait for the boot service to come up */
-        if (!srv_boot(*lgn, cdata->backend.data())) {
+        /* a service manager already told to stop (srv_pid is -1 once the
+         * last session has closed) reports nothing: its readiness would
+         * release a new login, held in srv_pending, against the services
+         * that are being stopped, and the next manager reports again
+         */
+        if (lgn->srv_pid == -1) {
+            print_dbg("pipe: manager stopping, readiness dropped");
+        } else if (!srv_boot(*lgn, cdata->backend.data())) {
             /* this is an unrecoverable condition */
             return false;
         }
